select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b11-005","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"How much solar energy does India receive, and what solar power capacity can a square kilometre of land support?","back":"About 5–7 kWh/m² per day for 300–330 days a year, enough to set up about 20 MW of solar power per km² of land.","explain":"Solar energy is used through two routes: solar thermal (sun's heat to heat energy) and solar photovoltaic (sunlight to electricity).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p302","order":1519},
{"id":"p1-b11-006","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What temperature does a flat plate collector reach, how is it built, and why does its output vary by season?","back":"About 40–60°C. Copper tubes welded to copper sheets, both with a highly absorbing black coating, with a toughened glass cover on top and insulation below, all in a flat box. Its efficiency depends strongly on ambient temperature, so output is higher in summer than in winter.","explain":"A solar water heating system consists of a flat plate or evacuated tube collector, an insulated storage tank (water stays hot overnight) and connecting pipes, with the collector facing the sun.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p302","order":1520},
{"id":"p1-b11-007","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"How is an evacuated tube collector built, what temperature can it reach, and how do its heat losses compare with a flat plate collector?","back":"Two concentric glass tubes fused at the ends with a vacuum between them; the inner tube has a selective absorbing coating, and water flows in through an innermost feeder tube. It reaches up to about 150°C. Heat loss is under 10%, vs about 40% for a flat plate collector.","explain":"Because it is insulated like a thermos flask, it needs no separate cover sheet or insulated box, and its efficiency does not drop with ambient temperature the way a flat plate collector's does.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p303","order":1521},
{"id":"p1-b11-008","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Why is an evacuated tube collector more efficient than a flat plate collector?","back":"The vacuum stops heat loss by conduction (and convection) back to the atmosphere, and the selective coating converts short-wave radiation to long-wave, preventing re-radiation. More heat is trapped, so it is far less sensitive to ambient temperature.","explain":"This is a standard short question (S-5). Sunlight passes through the clear outer tube, the coated inner tube absorbs it, and the trapped long-wave heat cannot escape through the vacuum.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p303","order":1522},
{"id":"p1-b11-009","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Describe how a solar power tower works, including the molten salt temperatures.","back":"A field of heliostats (mirrors) focuses sunlight onto a receiver on a tall tower. Molten salt from the cold tank is heated to about 566°C in the receiver and stored in a hot tank. It then passes through a steam generator to drive a turbine, and returns at about 288°C to the cold tank.","explain":"The hot and cold salt tanks give thermal storage, so generation can continue when the sun is not shining. The power tower and parabolic trough are the two basic types of solar thermal power station.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p304","order":1523},
{"id":"p1-b11-010","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is the composition of the molten salt used in solar power towers, and why is it preferred?","back":"60% sodium nitrate and 40% potassium nitrate. It is an efficient, low-cost thermal storage medium and is non-flammable and non-toxic.","explain":"Storing heat in molten salt decouples heat collection from electricity generation in a power tower plant.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p305","order":1524},
{"id":"p1-b11-011","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"How does a parabolic trough collector work? Give its concentration ratio and fluid temperature.","back":"Parabolic trough-shaped reflectors focus sunlight onto a receiver tube along their focal line, concentrating it 30–60 times and heating the fluid to about 400°C to raise steam for a turbine. The troughs lie on an east–west axis and rotate to track the sun.","explain":"The book calls it currently the most proven solar thermal electric technology. Such plants can produce many MW but are confined to areas with sufficient solar insolation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p305","order":1525},
{"id":"p1-b11-012","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is the photovoltaic effect, and why are solar cells 'tuned' and given an anti-reflective coating?","back":"Photons striking two dissimilar materials in close contact knock electrons out of their normal state, producing a voltage. Only photons of the right energy do this, so cells are tuned in manufacture to the most intense part of the sunlight spectrum. Silicon is naturally reflective, so an anti-reflective coating cuts reflection losses.","explain":"Because a cell cannot use all wavelengths, solar cell efficiency is low (the book quotes typical efficiencies of 10–15%). Besides silicon, cells are made of gallium arsenide, copper indium diselenide and cadmium telluride.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p305","order":1526},
{"id":"p1-b11-013","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"How many silicon cells make a standard PV module, why that number, and what does 'peak watt (Wp)' mean?","back":"36 cells (about 0.5 V each), enough voltage to charge a 12 V battery and run a pump or motor. Wp is the maximum power a module can deliver under standard test conditions; single modules range from about 5 to 120 Wp.","explain":"Cells are connected in series and parallel to form modules, and modules form arrays. A PV system comprises panels, a battery, a charge controller and an inverter for AC loads.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p306","order":1527},
{"id":"p1-b11-014","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Write the formula for the energy conversion efficiency of a PV panel.","back":"η (%) = Pm / (E × A) × 100, where Pm = maximum power output (W), E = insolation (W/m²) and A = cell or panel area (m²).","explain":"It measures how much of the incident solar energy becomes electricity. Commercial silicon panels typically fall in the 10–15% range quoted in the book.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p307","order":1528},
{"id":"p1-b11-015","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"Find the energy conversion efficiency of a 175 W solar panel measuring 0.75 m × 1.50 m when insolation is 1,000 W/m².","back":"Area = 1.125 m²; η = 175/(1.125 × 1000) × 100 ≈ 15.6%.","explain":"The panel converts 15.6% of the available solar energy into electricity. The rest is reflected or lost as heat, or is at wavelengths the cell cannot use.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p307","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the panel area.","answer":"0.75 × 1.50 = 1.125 m².","explain":"Area intercepting the sunlight."},{"prompt":"Find the incident solar power.","answer":"1.125 m² × 1000 W/m² = 1125 W.","explain":"E × A is the input power."},{"prompt":"Divide output by input.","answer":"175/1125 × 100 ≈ 15.6%.","explain":"η = Pm/(E × A) × 100."}],"order":1529},
{"id":"p1-b11-016","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"According to Table 11.1, how suitable for power generation are average wind speeds of 4, 5, 6, 7 and 8 m/s?","back":"Up to 4 m/s: no good; 5 m/s: poor; 6 m/s: moderate; 7 m/s: good; 8 m/s: excellent.","explain":"In km/h these are about 15, 18, 22, 25 and 29. Tropics have steady moderate winds all year, while temperate latitudes vary more but have more high-wind occurrences.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p309","order":1530},
{"id":"p1-b11-017","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Why are three-bladed rotors preferred for power generation, and where are multi-bladed rotors used?","back":"Three-bladed rotors run more smoothly and generally more quietly than two-bladed ones. Multi-bladed rotors have a large starting torque in light winds and are used for water pumping and low-frequency mechanical power.","explain":"Most turbines are horizontal-axis machines with the rotor shaft and generator on top of the tower, pointed into the wind; the rotor can be upwind or downwind of the tower. Blade shape and angle to the relative wind set the aerodynamic performance.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p309","order":1531},
{"id":"p1-b11-018","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"In a wind turbine nacelle, what are the typical speeds of the low-speed and high-speed shafts, and which generator is most common?","back":"Low-speed (main) shaft: about 30–60 rpm; the gearbox raises this to about 1,000–1,800 rpm on the high-speed shaft. The most common commercial generator is an induction generator producing 50-cycle AC.","explain":"The nacelle houses the gearbox, generator and controls. A disc brake on the main shaft or high-speed shaft slows the rotor, and yaw control (sensors plus a yaw motor) turns the nacelle to keep an upwind rotor aligned with the wind.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p310","order":1532},
{"id":"p1-b11-019","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Define cut-in, rated and cut-out (furling) wind speeds, with typical values.","back":"Cut-in: minimum speed for reliable useful power, around 5 m/s. Rated: lowest speed at which rated (maximum) power is reached, often 1.5 × the site mean wind speed. Cut-out (furling): speed above which the turbine shuts down to prevent damage, about 20–30 m/s depending on manufacturer.","explain":"Together with power output and capacity factor, these are the key operating characteristics shown on a turbine's power curve. The rated speed is chosen to fit the local wind regime.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p311","order":1533},
{"id":"p1-b11-020","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is the Betz limit, and what fraction of wind energy is actually converted to useful power?","back":"The theoretical maximum fraction of wind energy a rotor can capture, about 59%. Allowing for generator and gearbox losses, only about 15–25% becomes useful power.","explain":"A rotor cannot be 100% efficient because some air must pass through the blades for the turbine to turn; it extracts energy by slowing the wind. The blade coefficient of performance Cp generally ranges from 0.33 to 0.59.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p311","order":1534},
{"id":"p1-b11-021","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Write the formula for electrical power from a wind turbine, and state what doubling the wind speed vs doubling the swept area does.","back":"P = 0.5 × ρ × A × Cp × ηg × ηb × V³ (ρ air density, A swept area, Cp blade coefficient of performance, ηg generator and ηb gearbox efficiency, V wind speed). Doubling V gives 8× power; doubling A only 2×.","explain":"It comes from kinetic energy = ½mV² with mass flow = ρAV. Because of the cube law, siting turbines in the highest-wind areas is critical for good economics.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p312","order":1535},
{"id":"p1-b11-022","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"A wind turbine has a 6 m diameter rotor, Cp = 0.30, generator efficiency 0.8, gearbox efficiency 0.90 and wind speed 11 m/s. Taking air density as about 1.2 kg/m³, find the expected power output.","back":"A = π/4 × 6² ≈ 28.26 m²; P = 0.5 × 1.2 × 28.26 × 0.30 × 0.8 × 0.90 × 11³ ≈ 4,875 W (4.875 kW).","explain":"This is the book's worked example (the 1.2 kg/m³ density is consistent with its 4,875 W answer). The V³ term dominates: 11³ = 1331.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p312","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the swept area.","answer":"A = π/4 × 6² = 28.26 m².","explain":"Area of the circle swept by the rotor."},{"prompt":"Multiply the efficiency terms.","answer":"Cp × ηg × ηb = 0.30 × 0.8 × 0.90 = 0.216.","explain":"Combined blade, generator and gearbox efficiency."},{"prompt":"Cube the wind speed.","answer":"11³ = 1331 m³/s³.","explain":"Power varies with V³."},{"prompt":"Compute power.","answer":"P = 0.5 × 1.2 × 28.26 × 0.216 × 1331 ≈ 4,875 W = 4.875 kW.","explain":"P = 0.5ρA·Cp·ηg·ηb·V³."}],"order":1536},
{"id":"p1-b11-023","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Define the capacity factor of a wind turbine and give its typical range.","back":"CF = actual kWh produced in a period (usually a year) ÷ (8760 × nameplate rating in kW). Typically 20–40%, at the upper end only at particularly favourable sites.","explain":"It compares actual output with the theoretical maximum if the turbine ran at full rating all year. The variability of the wind is the main reason it is well below 100%.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p312","order":1537},
{"id":"p1-b11-024","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"A 2.5 MW wind turbine actually produced 5,000,000 kWh in a year. Find its capacity factor.","back":"Theoretical maximum = 2.5 × 1000 × 8760 = 21,900,000 kWh; CF = 5,000,000/21,900,000 ≈ 22.8%.","explain":"The book's example shows that a turbine's real annual output is far below nameplate × hours because of wind variability. 22.8% is within the typical 20–40% range.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p313","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert rating to kW.","answer":"2.5 MW = 2500 kW.","explain":"Keep units consistent with kWh."},{"prompt":"Find theoretical maximum annual output.","answer":"2500 × 8760 = 21,900,000 kWh.","explain":"Full rating for all 8760 hours of the year."},{"prompt":"Divide actual by theoretical.","answer":"5,000,000/21,900,000 ≈ 0.228 = 22.8%.","explain":"CF = kWh produced/(8760 × kW rating)."}],"order":1538},
{"id":"p1-b11-025","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Why is biomass considered 'carbon neutral'?","back":"The CO₂ released when biomass is burned is largely balanced by the CO₂ absorbed during its growth through photosynthesis.","explain":"Biomass is effectively stored solar energy. It includes wood, straw, crops, algae, sewage sludge and animal and other biological waste; low-density biomass is processed into pellets or briquettes for direct combustion in grate, stoker or fluidised-bed systems.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p313","order":1539},
{"id":"p1-b11-026","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is biomass gasification? Give its temperature, the calorific value of producer gas and the typical conversion efficiency.","back":"Partial combustion of biomass with less than stoichiometric air at about 1000°C, giving combustible CO and H₂ (traces of CH₄) plus tar and dust. Producer gas CV is 1000–1200 kcal/Nm³; conversion efficiency is 60–70%.","explain":"Complete combustion would give only CO₂ and water. The key to gasifier design is to reduce biomass to charcoal and then convert the charcoal at a suitable temperature into CO and H₂. Used in a dual-fuel DG set, producer gas can save 65–85% of diesel.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p314","order":1540},
{"id":"p1-b11-027","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four zones of a biomass gasifier in the order biomass passes through them, and the four main stages of a gasification system.","back":"Zones: drying (distillation), pyrolysis, combustion, reduction. System stages: feeding of feedstock, gasifier reactions, cleaning of the gas, utilisation of the cleaned gas.","explain":"Exothermic reactions in the combustion zone (C + O₂ → CO₂; H₂ + ½O₂ → H₂O) supply the heat. In the reduction zone over hot charcoal: C + CO₂ → 2CO and C + H₂O → CO + H₂ (reduction), CO₂ + H₂ → CO + H₂O (water gas), and C + 2H₂ → CH₄ (methanation).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p314","order":1541},
{"id":"p1-b11-028","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"1 kg of dry wood (average CV 3500 kcal/kg) produces 2.5 m³ of producer gas with an average CV of 1000 kcal/Nm³. Find the gasifier conversion efficiency.","back":"η = (2.5 × 1000)/3500 × 100 ≈ 71%.","explain":"Gasifier efficiency = calorific value of gas produced per kg of fuel ÷ calorific value of 1 kg of fuel. The 29% balance is lost as sensible heat, tar, char and dust.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p315","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the heat in the gas per kg of wood.","answer":"2.5 m³ × 1000 kcal/m³ = 2500 kcal.","explain":"Gas volume × gas CV."},{"prompt":"Identify the heat input per kg of wood.","answer":"3500 kcal.","explain":"CV of the dry wood."},{"prompt":"Divide output by input.","answer":"2500/3500 × 100 ≈ 71%.","explain":"Within the 60–70%+ range typical of gasifiers."}],"order":1542},
{"id":"p1-b11-029","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"20 kg of wood (CV 3200 kcal/kg) produces 46 m³ of producer gas with an average CV of 1000 kcal/Nm³. Find the gasifier's conversion efficiency.","back":"Heat input 64,000 kcal; heat output 46,000 kcal; efficiency ≈ 71.88%.","explain":"This is the chapter's solved example. It uses the same principle as the per-kg formula, just with total quantities.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p326","steps":[{"prompt":"Calculate heat input.","answer":"20 kg × 3200 kcal/kg = 64,000 kcal.","explain":"Mass of wood × its CV."},{"prompt":"Calculate heat output in the gas.","answer":"46 m³ × 1000 kcal/m³ = 46,000 kcal.","explain":"Gas volume × gas CV."},{"prompt":"Calculate efficiency.","answer":"46,000/64,000 × 100 = 71.88%.","explain":"Efficiency = heat output/heat input."}],"order":1543},
{"id":"p1-b11-030","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Can a diesel (CI) engine run entirely on producer gas? Compare biomass and diesel use per kWh for CI and spark-ignition engines.","back":"No. A CI engine needs pilot diesel: about 1 kg of biomass plus 0.07 litres of diesel per kWh, saving 80–85% diesel. A spark-ignition engine on producer gas gives about 0.55–0.75 kWh per kg of biomass.","explain":"CI engines rely on compression ignition, and producer gas cannot ignite that way alone, so a small diesel charge is injected (dual-fuel mode). SI engines use a spark, so they can run fully on producer gas.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p315","order":1544},
{"id":"p1-b11-031","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is biomethanation, what does gobar gas consist of, and what extra advantage does this process have?","back":"Anaerobic (oxygen-free) bacterial digestion of biomass (dung, sewage sludge, MSW, food, distillery and other biodegradable wastes) into biogas. Gobar gas is about 60% methane and 40% CO₂. It is the only biomass process that also gives high-grade manure as a by-product.","explain":"Biogas gives higher useful energy from dung than burning it directly: in the book's chart, 25 kg of fresh dung gives about 2,592 kcal of useful energy as biogas vs about 1,046 kcal by burning 5 kg of dried dung. Bio-methane can fully replace natural gas in boilers, furnaces and IC engines.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p315","order":1545},
{"id":"p1-b11-032","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Describe the four stages of anaerobic digestion.","back":"1) Acidic bacteria break complex organic molecules into smaller ones; 2) these break down further into organic acids, CO₂ and ammonia; 3) methanogenic bacteria convert them into acetates and hydrogen; 4) these are converted to methane.","explain":"Methane-producing bacteria are very sensitive to ambient conditions, which can slow or stop the process. In a floating-drum plant, a non-return valve keeps air out, since air would kill the bacteria and could create an explosive mixture.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p316","order":1546},
{"id":"p1-b11-033","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"How are ethanol and biodiesel produced, and how are they used?","back":"Ethanol: fermentation of molasses or any carbohydrate-rich feedstock (sugar beet, sweet corn, ligno-cellulosic straw and wood waste); used as a fuel additive to cut CO and smog emissions, and flexible-fuel vehicles use up to 85% ethanol. Biodiesel: transesterification of plant oil (e.g. non-edible Jatropha) with an alcohol such as methanol; used as a blend (typically 20%) or neat in diesel engines.","explain":"Biodiesel can be made from any plant oil, waste cooking oil or animal fat (exam answer: animal and vegetable fat). Jatropha grows on dry, arid land and is non-edible, so it does not compete with food.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p317","order":1547},
{"id":"p1-b11-034","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Classify hydropower by size according to Table 11.2.","back":"Large: >25 MW, grid-fed. Small: 2,001 kW–25 MW, usually grid-fed. Mini: 101 kW–2 MW, stand-alone or grid. Micro: 11–100 kW, usually for remote off-grid areas. Pico: a few hundred watts up to 10 kW.","explain":"A run-of-the-river micro-hydro scheme needs no storage. It diverts part of the river through an intake weir and settling basin, along a channel to a forebay tank, then down a penstock to the turbine in the power house.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p318","order":1548},
{"id":"p1-b11-035","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Write the formula for the power potential of falling water, and what overall efficiency does the book suggest for small hydro systems?","back":"Theoretical P (kW) = 9.81 × Q × H, with Q in m³/s and H in m. For small systems of a few kW, take overall efficiency as about 50%.","explain":"Small turbines rarely exceed 80% efficiency, and there are further friction losses in the penstock, so the theoretical power is multiplied by about 0.5 for a realistic figure.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p319","order":1549},
{"id":"p1-b11-036","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"A turbine-generator operates at a head of 10 m with a flow of 0.3 m³/s. Taking 50% overall efficiency, how much electricity will it deliver?","back":"P = 9.81 × 0.3 × 10 × 0.5 ≈ 14.7 kW.","explain":"The theoretical hydraulic power is 29.43 kW. Applying the book's rule-of-thumb 50% overall efficiency for small systems gives about 14.7 kW of electricity.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p319","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the formula.","answer":"P = 9.81 × Q × H × η (kW).","explain":"Q in m³/s, H in m."},{"prompt":"Compute theoretical power.","answer":"9.81 × 0.3 × 10 = 29.43 kW.","explain":"Before losses."},{"prompt":"Apply efficiency.","answer":"29.43 × 0.5 = 14.715 kW ≈ 14.7 kW.","explain":"50% overall is typical for small schemes."}],"order":1550},
{"id":"p1-b11-037","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"problem","front":"How much power can a run-of-river mini hydro plant generate with a flow of 20 litres/s and a head of 12 m, assuming 60% system efficiency?","back":"Q = 0.02 m³/s; P = 9.81 × 0.02 × 12 × 0.6 ≈ 1.4 kW.","explain":"The common mistake is forgetting to convert litres/s to m³/s (divide by 1000). The book writes it as 12 × 20 × 9.81 × 0.6/1000 = 1.4 kW.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p319","steps":[{"prompt":"Convert flow to m³/s.","answer":"20 L/s = 0.02 m³/s.","explain":"1 m³ = 1000 litres."},{"prompt":"Compute theoretical power.","answer":"9.81 × 0.02 × 12 = 2.354 kW.","explain":"P = 9.81QH."},{"prompt":"Apply system efficiency.","answer":"2.354 × 0.6 ≈ 1.41 kW.","explain":"Given system efficiency is 60%."}],"order":1551},
{"id":"p1-b11-038","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Explain how a fuel cell produces electricity.","back":"Hydrogen enters the anode, where a catalyst splits it into protons and electrons. The electrolyte lets protons pass to the cathode but not electrons, which flow through an external circuit as current. At the cathode, oxygen combines with the protons and returning electrons to form water and heat.","explain":"Since the conversion is electrochemical, not combustion, it is clean, quiet and highly efficient. Cells are stacked in series. Hydrogen is a secondary energy source (from steam reforming of natural gas, electrolysis or biomass gasification), and its high production cost is the biggest hurdle to large-scale use.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p319","order":1552},
{"id":"p1-b11-039","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Match each fuel cell type to its operating temperature: PEMFC, DMFC, PAFC, AFC, MCFC, SOFC.","back":"PEMFC: low temperature (high-temperature variants up to 200°C). DMFC: 60–130°C. PAFC: about 180°C. AFC: about 70°C. MCFC: about 650°C. SOFC: about 800–1,000°C.","explain":"Electrolytes: PEMFC and DMFC use polymer membranes; PAFC liquid phosphoric acid in a silicon carbide matrix; AFC aqueous potassium hydroxide; MCFC molten carbonate salt in a ceramic matrix; SOFC solid ceramic (stabilised zirconia). MCFC and SOFC run on synthesis gas or methane and are best run continuously.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p320","order":1553},
{"id":"p1-b11-040","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Which fuel cell type is used for each: NASA space shuttles; most megawatt-scale fuel cell power plants; portable devices under 250 W; vehicles; 100–400 kW stationary generators?","back":"NASA shuttles: alkaline (AFC). MW-scale plants and large CHP: molten carbonate (MCFC). Portable <250 W: direct methanol (DMFC). Vehicles: PEMFC (variable output, high power density). 100–400 kW stationary: phosphoric acid (PAFC).","explain":"PAFC is quite resistant to CO poisoning and can exceed 80% overall efficiency if the heat is used. AFC needs pure hydrogen and oxygen because it is very sensitive to poisoning. Overall, fuel cells span about 1 W to 10 MW.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p321","order":1554},
{"id":"p1-b11-041","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is landfill gas, and how is it recovered for energy?","back":"Biogas (mainly methane and CO₂) formed by natural anaerobic decomposition of organic matter in landfills. Perforated pipes inserted into the landfill collect it under natural pressure for use as fuel, e.g. in a gas engine-generator.","explain":"If not captured, landfill methane escapes and adds to greenhouse emissions. In a waste-to-energy direct combustion plant, the remaining ash takes about a tenth of the original space of the trash.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p322","order":1555},
{"id":"p1-b11-042","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"Compare wave energy with wind energy: energy content, dependence on height or speed, and power per km of coast.","back":"Waves hold roughly 1000 times the kinetic energy of wind. Wave power varies with the square of wave height (wind with the cube of speed), and water is 850 times denser than air. About 40 MW/km of coast is possible with 1 m waves and 1000 MW/km with 5 m waves.","explain":"Waves travel long distances with little loss, so wave power is steadier and more predictable than wind or solar, and runs round the clock. In an oscillating chamber, the rising wave pushes air through a turbine, and the falling wave draws air back, so power is generated both ways.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p323","order":1556},
{"id":"p1-b11-043","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"What is the basic requirement for tapping tidal energy, and what causes tides?","back":"A tidal height difference of at least 5 m. Tides are caused by the combined gravitational pull of the moon and sun.","explain":"High and low tides occur twice a day, and bays and inlets amplify them. Water trapped behind a dam at high tide is released through turbines as the tide falls, like a hydro plant; turbines can generate as the tide comes in and as it goes out.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p324","order":1557},
{"id":"p1-b11-044","paper":1,"topic":"New & renewable energy sources","kind":"fact","front":"How fast does underground temperature rise with depth, and at roughly what depth is rock hot enough to boil water? What is the difference between magma and lava?","back":"About 3°C per 100 m, so rock is hot enough to boil water at about 3000 m. Magma is the hot liquid rock in the upper mantle; when it breaks through the surface in a volcano, it is called lava.","explain":"Water reaching hot rock can exceed 148°C; where it comes up through a crack it forms a hot spring. A geothermal power plant is like a regular plant except that no fuel is burned to make steam.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 11 p325","order":1558}
]$cards$::jsonb);
